WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:10.380
Unix has its own unique way of handling processes, and understanding it will help in creating scripts and debugging problems.

2
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.380
Starting from the top and working our way down, a process is an instance of a running program.

3
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:21.380
A process can kick off other processes, which can in turn kick off other additional processes.

4
00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:25.380
If you were to chart all this out, it would kind of look like a family tree.

5
00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.380
And those processes are called parent processes and child processes.

6
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:38.380
So a parent process creates child processes, and that organization of parent processes and child processes is called a process tree.

7
00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:47.380
When a parent process spawns a child process, that parent process can share some, none, or all of its resources with the child process.

8
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:53.380
The parent process can run alongside the child process, or it can wait until the child process is done.

9
00:00:53.380 --> 00:01:00.380
In Unix, the command to see the processes that are running is ps, short for process status.

10
00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:08.380
The bpx batch utility gives you a way of running Unix scripts and applications as zos batch jobs.

11
00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:13.380
There's two ways of invoking this, as JCL and as a tso command.

12
00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.380
You can see here, that looks like Unix stuff to me.

13
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:21.380
We've got a shell script here and dd statements for the output and error streams over here.

14
00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:24.380
You can also run bpx batch as a tso command.

15
00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:31.380
Here's one example, the sh tells bpx batch to start a shell and run shell commands or scripts.

16
00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:38.380
So if you want to run a script and then redirect the output to do something you'd normally type into the shell, that's where you'd do that.

17
00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:43.380
You can also use bpx batch pgm, which is for a program.

18
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.380
It will kick off a program in what's called a spawn or a fork and exec.

19
00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:53.380
It takes the parameters you specify, which are separated by spaces, and sends them into the program.

20
00:01:53.380 --> 00:02:01.380
So not only can you use bpx batch to do Unix from zos, there's a few different ways you can do it depending on your needs.

21
00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:06.380
You've seen in the JCL example that there are three types of files that a running program uses.

22
00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:11.380
There's standard input, standard output, and standard error.

23
00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:17.380
Input is any source of input, which could be from the keyboard or it could be the output from another program.

24
00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:22.380
Standard output is where a program typically writes out its progress and informational messages,

25
00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:28.380
and standard error is where you'd look for details on why a program isn't running quite right.

26
00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.380
Hopefully you don't have to spend too much time looking in there.

27
00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:36.380
One thing you'll have to keep track of in Unix is users, groups, and permissions.

28
00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.380
Each user has a user ID or UID.

29
00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:47.380
On a zos system with rackf, part of your rackf profile contains the information about your USS user ID,

30
00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:52.380
what directory all of your stuff is in, what type of shell you like to use, all that kind of stuff.

31
00:02:52.380 --> 00:03:00.380
And a user who is logged in can also issue the switch user command, su, pronounced sue, to become another user.

32
00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:04.380
They of course need permission to do that and they might have to enter the password to switch,

33
00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:09.380
but that really helps when you've set something up for another user and you want to try it out as them before you tell them,

34
00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:11.380
it's all good, ready to go.

35
00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:15.380
In addition to user IDs, there are also group IDs.

36
00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:21.380
The idea there being that you can have a bunch of users in a group that have access to some of the same files,

37
00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:24.380
but not necessarily all of the same files.

38
00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:30.380
So about that whole access thing, when you look at a thing, and I'm being purposely vague here,

39
00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:35.380
but when you look at a thing in Unix, you get back something that looks like this.

40
00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:38.380
The first character says what that thing is.

41
00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:44.380
It could be a file, in which case you'd see a dash, it could be a directory, in which case you'll see a D,

42
00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:49.380
or a symbolic link, a type of shortcut or pointer, which shows up as an L.

43
00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:52.380
Then you get this series of letters and dashes.

44
00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:57.380
The letters are R, W, and X, which stand for read, write, and execute.

45
00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:02.380
And it determines whether the file can be read, write, or executed, run as a program.

46
00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:04.380
And there's three sets of these letters.

47
00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:08.380
The first set is saying what the owner of the file can do with it.

48
00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:12.380
It only makes sense that a user would have read and write access to their own file,

49
00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:17.380
though they probably wouldn't have execute access unless it's a program or a script they want to run.

50
00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:19.380
The next section is for the group.

51
00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:22.380
A file is also associated with a group.

52
00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.380
And I might have a file that my coworkers need to read, but I don't want them to write to it.

53
00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:29.380
So I can set that up with just read access for them.

54
00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:32.380
The last group is for other, for everybody else.

55
00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:38.380
If I want other people who aren't me, or people who aren't specifically in that group to have some sort of access,

56
00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:41.380
I set those permissions here.

57
00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:43.380
Good job, guys. Take five.

58
00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:47.810
These are the permissions for files.

59
00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:51.810
They look the same for directories, but with a slightly different meaning.

60
00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:56.810
Read means that you can see the contents, write means you can add or delete things,

61
00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:59.810
and execute means you can go into the directory.

62
00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:02.810
Sometimes it makes sense to represent permissions numerically.

63
00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:06.810
You'll see this a lot of times when you're setting permissions from the command line.

64
00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:08.810
Each permission has a value.

65
00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:13.810
Read is four, write is two, execute is one.

66
00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:19.810
You figure out what permissions you want, if any, add them together, and that's it.

67
00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:22.810
Here's what all the combinations look like.

68
00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:28.810
So if I have a file where I need full access, including read, write, and execute,

69
00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:32.810
and no one else gets anything, it would be 700.

70
00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:36.810
Another example, if I have a file that I can read, write, and execute,

71
00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:41.810
the specified group members can read and execute, but not write,

72
00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:45.810
and everybody else can only read, that would be...

73
00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:48.810
You know what? Why don't you tell me?

74
00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:50.810
Pop quiz time!

75
00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:52.810
The answer is seven, five, four.

76
00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:57.810
Seven because that's everything, plus it's four plus two plus one.

77
00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:03.810
Five for group because that's read four plus execute one equals five.

78
00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:08.810
And for others, they get four because they can read it, but nothing else.

79
00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:12.810
Here's a listing of some other common user group other permissions.

80
00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:17.810
These will come in handy, so, you know, maybe take a screenshot or something.

81
00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:20.810
If you have a file and you want to change the permissions on it,

82
00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:23.810
use the chmod command, chmod.

83
00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:27.810
You say chmod, the numerical permission, followed by the file name.

84
00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:33.810
You can also do it in one section at a time by typing chmod g plus w, for example,

85
00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:35.810
to give write access to the group.

86
00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:37.810
But be careful what permissions you give out.

87
00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:42.810
When you give permissions to others, so someone else not in the group can have access to a file,

88
00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:46.810
you're giving those permissions to literally everybody else on the system.

89
00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:50.810
If you're working with people you need to share access with, consider using a group.

90
00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:53.810
That's literally what they're there for.

91
00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:56.810
Like I said, Unix is its own beast.

92
00:06:56.810 --> 00:07:01.810
And like zos, there's no single spot that marks when you've learned all there is to learn.

93
00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:05.810
But we have hit a pretty good spot right here where at least you're prepared

94
00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:08.810
to get started with Unix system services on zos.

95
00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:11.810
So that brings this lesson to a close.
